21:06:13 [00a9] 3. END: ethan achieves the goal, learns something, and feels positive about the experience.
21:06:13 [00a9] 
21:06:13 [00a9] Therapy goals to integrate naturally into the story (do NOT list them explicitly — weave them into narrative, dialogue, and action):
21:06:13 [00a9] 
21:06:13 [00a9] - Learning descriptive words (adjectives, spatial terms)
21:06:13 [00a9] - Collaboration and turn-taking
21:06:13 [00a9] - Importance of friendships and relationships
21:06:13 [00a9] - Overcoming challenges with support
21:06:13 [00a9] 
21:06:13 [00a9] --- LANGUAGE & INTEREST GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
21:06:13 [00a9] Target developmental level: age 8, approx MLU 11+ words per utterance. Keep sentences at or near this length.
21:06:13 [00a9] 
21:06:13 [00a9] Weave these language targets naturally into narration and dialogue (model them in context; do not drill or quiz them in the story):
21:06:13 [00a9] - irregular past tense (Irregular past tense verbs): e.g. went, ate, saw, gave
21:06:13 [00a9] - advanced conjunction (More complex conjunction structures): e.g. while, although, before, after
21:06:13 [00a9] - auxiliary verbs (Helping verbs): e.g. is, are, was, were, has, have
21:06:13 [00a9] - copula verbs (Linking verbs): e.g. is, am, are, was, were
21:06:13 [00a9] - verb tense concepts (Past, present, future, and progressive tense usage): e.g. I play, I played, I will play, I am playing
21:06:13 [00a9] - causal explanation language (Language forms used to explain cause-effect relationships.): e.g. because, so, if...then, that happened because...
21:06:13 [00a9] - narrative sequence language (Language forms used to organize story events and sequences.): e.g. first, then, next, after that, finally
21:06:13 [00a9] - perspective taking language (Language forms used to discuss thoughts, feelings, beliefs, and perspectives.): e.g. he thinks, she feels, they know, I wonder, maybe
21:06:13 [00a9] 
21:06:13 [00a9] Practise these target speech sounds by naturally featuring words that contain them (do not turn the story into a pronunciation drill):
21:06:13 [00a9] - advanced sounds [th, r, ch] (Late-developing speech sounds for articulation practice.): e.g. thumb, three, rabbit, red, chair, cheese
21:06:13 [00a9] 
21:06:13 [00a9] Build the story around this interest theme — use it for the story's hook, characters, and setting:
21:06:13 [00a9] - video game characters (game hero, game creature, avatar, player character, helper character, monster, treasure, level, quest, race, power-up)
21:06:13 [00a9] 
21:06:13 [00a9] --- TAKEAWAYS / LESSONS ---
21:06:13 [00a9] The story MUST teach 2–3 clear takeaways that the child can articulate after listening. Demonstrate them through what characters DO and the consequences they face — do NOT preach or moralise inside the narration. After the story ends, list the takeaways explicitly in a ** Takeaways ** section.
21:06:13 [00a9] 
21:06:13 [00a9] Takeaway rules:
21:06:13 [00a9] - Each takeaway is ONE short sentence a 7-year-old can repeat back in their own words.
21:06:13 [00a9] - Phrase them as positive, actionable lessons or values — not as "do not" prohibitions when a positive form is natural.
21:06:13 [00a9] - Each takeaway must connect to a specific moment or decision in the story (cause → consequence).
21:06:13 [00a9] - Cover values like honesty, kindness, perseverance, asking for help, sharing, courage, patience, listening, or fairness — pick the ones that fit the plot.
21:06:13 [00a9] 
21:06:13 [00a9] Examples of well-formed takeaways:
21:06:13 [00a9] - "Asking for help is a sign of being smart, not weak."
21:06:13 [00a9] - "Being patient often works better than rushing."
21:06:13 [00a9] - "Telling the truth is hard but builds trust."
21:06:13 [00a9] - "Treating others kindly makes them want to help you back."
21:06:13 [00a9] --- TONE AND STYLE ---
21:06:13 [00a9] - Warm, encouraging, and gently paced.
21:06:13 [00a9] - Show, don't tell: use actions and dialogue to convey emotions rather than stating them.
21:06:13 [00a9] - Include at least one moment of humor, wonder, or sensory delight.
21:06:13 [00a9] - Use character names consistently (avoid pronoun ambiguity for young readers).
21:06:13 [00a9] 
