21:16:19 [aeac] --- AGE-APPROPRIATE LANGUAGE REQUIREMENTS ---
21:16:19 [aeac] Language level: Use complex sentences with subordinate clauses. Include nuanced emotional and social vocabulary (empathy, compromise, perseverance). Introduce figurative language gently (similes, simple metaphors) with clear context. Model inferencing and perspective-taking through character thoughts and dialogue. Story structure: Three-act structure with internal conflict and character growth. Characters: Realistic motivations and interpersonal dynamics. Dialogue: Extended exchanges showing negotiation, repair, and social problem-solving.
21:16:19 [aeac] 
21:16:19 [aeac] --- STORY SETTING AND STRUCTURE ---
21:16:19 [aeac] Set the story in a familiar, child-friendly environment.
21:16:19 [aeac] The obstacle should involve a manageable challenge that requires help from others.
21:16:19 [aeac] The resolution should leave the protagonist feeling proud, grateful, and connected.
21:16:19 [aeac] 
21:16:19 [aeac] Use a clear three-act structure:
21:16:19 [aeac] 1. BEGINNING: Introduce ethan, the setting, and ethan's goal or desire.
21:16:19 [aeac] 2. MIDDLE: ethan encounters an obstacle. ethan meets a supportive character who helps. Show the process of overcoming the challenge together.
21:16:19 [aeac] 3. END: ethan achieves the goal, learns something, and feels positive about the experience.
21:16:19 [aeac] 
21:16:19 [aeac] Therapy goals to integrate naturally into the story (do NOT list them explicitly — weave them into narrative, dialogue, and action):
21:16:19 [aeac] 
21:16:19 [aeac] - Learning descriptive words (adjectives, spatial terms)
21:16:19 [aeac] - Collaboration and turn-taking
21:16:19 [aeac] - Importance of friendships and relationships
21:16:19 [aeac] - Overcoming challenges with support
21:16:19 [aeac] 
21:16:19 [aeac] --- LANGUAGE & INTEREST GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
21:16:19 [aeac] Target developmental level: age 8, approx MLU 11+ words per utterance. Keep sentences at or near this length.
21:16:19 [aeac] 
21:16:19 [aeac] Weave these language targets naturally into narration and dialogue (model them in context; do not drill or quiz them in the story):
21:16:19 [aeac] - irregular past tense (Irregular past tense verbs): e.g. went, ate, saw, gave
21:16:19 [aeac] - advanced conjunction (More complex conjunction structures): e.g. while, although, before, after
21:16:19 [aeac] - auxiliary verbs (Helping verbs): e.g. is, are, was, were, has, have
21:16:19 [aeac] - copula verbs (Linking verbs): e.g. is, am, are, was, were
21:16:19 [aeac] - verb tense concepts (Past, present, future, and progressive tense usage): e.g. I play, I played, I will play, I am playing
21:16:19 [aeac] - causal explanation language (Language forms used to explain cause-effect relationships.): e.g. because, so, if...then, that happened because...
21:16:19 [aeac] - narrative sequence language (Language forms used to organize story events and sequences.): e.g. first, then, next, after that, finally
21:16:19 [aeac] - perspective taking language (Language forms used to discuss thoughts, feelings, beliefs, and perspectives.): e.g. he thinks, she feels, they know, I wonder, maybe
21:16:19 [aeac] 
21:16:19 [aeac] Practise these target speech sounds by naturally featuring words that contain them (do not turn the story into a pronunciation drill):
21:16:19 [aeac] - advanced sounds [th, r, ch] (Late-developing speech sounds for articulation practice.): e.g. thumb, three, rabbit, red, chair, cheese
21:16:19 [aeac] 
