21:22:04 [b9cd] [StoryGenerator] prompt:  Write a short story for a 5-year-old female named sophie, who has speech delay. The story will be used by a robot to practise WH-question comprehension (WHO / WHAT / WHERE) with the child.
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] --- STYLE REFERENCE (from the curated 4-to-7 corpus, WHO/WHAT/WHERE subset for this age) ---
21:22:04 [b9cd] Follow the general style, length, vocabulary, and structure of these examples. Each story is followed by 5–7 WH-questions whose answers are explicitly stated in the story.
21:22:04 [b9cd] Example 1 (setting: schoolyard):
21:22:04 [b9cd]   Story: The children are in the schoolyard. They are playing. The boys are throwing a football. The girls are jumping rope. Oh no, rain clouds are coming! Soon the children will have to go inside.
21:22:04 [b9cd]   Questions:
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHERE are the children?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHAT are they doing?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHO is playing football?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHAT are the girls doing?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHAT is coming?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHERE will the children go?
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] Example 2 (setting: bedroom):
21:22:04 [b9cd]   Story: The children are playing checkers on the rug. The girl loves checkers. One checker is missing. It is black. The children look for the checker. It is under the bed.
21:22:04 [b9cd]   Questions:
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHERE are the children?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHAT are they playing?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHO loves checkers?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHAT is missing?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHAT color is it?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHO looks for the checker?
21:22:04 [b9cd]   - WHERE is the checker?
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] --- END STYLE REFERENCE ---
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] --- AGE-APPROPRIATE LANGUAGE REQUIREMENTS ---
21:22:04 [b9cd] Language level: Write concrete sentences in the present tense. Use simple vocabulary a 4–5 year old already knows (ball, beach, dog, bus, kite, snow, library). Each sentence states a single observable fact (who is there, where, what is happening, what surprises them). Avoid abstract concepts, idioms, figurative language, and complex compound sentences. Story structure: One small scene — everyday or imaginative — with a tiny twist or surprise. Examples of scenes: building a sandcastle and finding a crab; flying a kite and the wind blowing it onto a dog; dropping toothpaste on the bathroom floor; spotting an unusual lunch in the cafeteria. Characters: 1–3 named characters; refer to them by name (not pronouns) so questions are answerable. Follow-up questions: After the story, generate 5–7 WH-questions (WHO/WHAT/WHERE ONLY — no HOW/WHY at this age) whose answers are explicitly stated in the story text — one fact per question, in roughly story order.
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] --- STORY SETTING AND THEME ---
21:22:04 [b9cd] Set the story in a familiar, child-friendly environment.
21:22:04 [b9cd] The obstacle should involve a manageable challenge that requires help from others.
21:22:04 [b9cd] The resolution should leave the protagonist feeling proud, grateful, and connected.
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] Therapy goals to integrate naturally into the story (do NOT list them explicitly — weave them into narrative, dialogue, and action):
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Learning descriptive words (adjectives, spatial terms)
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Collaboration and turn-taking
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Importance of friendships and relationships
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Overcoming challenges with support
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] --- LANGUAGE & INTEREST GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
21:22:04 [b9cd] Target developmental level: age 5, approx MLU 6-8 words per utterance. Keep sentences at or near this length.
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] Weave these language targets naturally into narration and dialogue (model them in context; do not drill or quiz them in the story):
21:22:04 [b9cd] - plural forms (Regular plural nouns): e.g. dogs, cars, apples, cats, toys
21:22:04 [b9cd] - ing form (Present participle/progressive verb form): e.g. running, jumping, eating, playing
21:22:04 [b9cd] - pronouns later (Later developing pronouns): e.g. he, she, they, her, his, their
21:22:04 [b9cd] - location noun (Location concept expressed with a noun phrase): e.g. in the box, on the table, under the chair
21:22:04 [b9cd] - simple conjunction (Early conjunction use): e.g. and, or
21:22:04 [b9cd] - intermediate conjunction (Pre-school conjunction use): e.g. because, but, so, if
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] Practise these target speech sounds by naturally featuring words that contain them (do not turn the story into a pronunciation drill):
21:22:04 [b9cd] - advanced sounds [th, r, ch] (Late-developing speech sounds for articulation practice.): e.g. thumb, three, rabbit, red, chair, cheese
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] Build the story around this interest theme — use it for the story's hook, characters, and setting:
21:22:04 [b9cd] - fairies (fairy, magic wand, wings)
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] --- TONE AND STYLE ---
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Warm, simple, and concrete. Describe one small scene — everyday or imaginative — with a tiny surprise or twist.
21:22:04 [b9cd] - sophie should appear by name in the story (not just "she" / "he"), so WHO-questions are answerable.
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Use observable facts only (who is there, where they are, what they are doing, what they see/find) — even imaginative characters must do concrete, observable things so the questions are answerable.
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Do NOT invent moral lessons, internal monologue, or three-act structure. Keep it to the corpus style.
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] Return your answer EXACTLY in this format, with no other text before or after:
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] ** Title **
21:22:04 [b9cd] <one short title>
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] <the full story text>
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] ** End **
21:22:04 [b9cd] ** Questions **
21:22:04 [b9cd] 1. <WH-question>
21:22:04 [b9cd] 2. <WH-question>
21:22:04 [b9cd] 3. <WH-question>
21:22:04 [b9cd] 4. <WH-question>
21:22:04 [b9cd] 5. <WH-question>
21:22:04 [b9cd] (6. and 7. optional)
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] ** Explanation of the output **
21:22:04 [b9cd] <1–2 short sentences explaining how the story and questions match the selected topic(s) and the learning goals: general speech-language therapy goals>
21:22:04 [b9cd] 
21:22:04 [b9cd] STRICT RULES:
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Do NOT include any preamble, commentary, or meta-text (no "Here is your story", "Sure!", etc.).
21:22:04 [b9cd] - The story text (between Title and End) MUST be between 70 and 100 words.
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Generate 5–7 WH-questions using ONLY WHO / WHAT / WHERE (no HOW / WHY at this age tier).
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Every question's answer MUST appear word-for-word in the story text. Do NOT ask about anything not stated.
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Refer to characters by name (not pronouns) in the story so questions like "WHO is in the story?" are answerable.
21:22:04 [b9cd] - Do NOT add sections beyond Title, story text, End, Questions, and Explanation.
